{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 110,
    "dDurationMs": 3039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d like to thank Speakly for supporting\nPBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3149,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we think of an exotic state of matter\nwe tend to think of the really weird things"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6359,
    "dDurationMs": 4571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that matter can do in extreme circumstances\n- like how at very high emperatures we get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10930,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the plasma that the sun is made of, or at\nextreme densities we get the nuclear matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15040,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of neutron stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16780,
    "dDurationMs": 3419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or in extreme cold we can have superconductors\nand superfluids."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20199,
    "dDurationMs": 4931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s one state of matter that’s\nnot solid, liquid or gas, but is also not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25130,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confined to extreme or rare environments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27510,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, there are planets in our solar system completely covered with this stuff,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33050,
    "dDurationMs": 5451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and you’ve often been the beneficiary of the powers of this state of matter without even knowing it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43835,
    "dDurationMs": 3055,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m sure you know how states of matter work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46890,
    "dDurationMs": 2510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heat a block of ice and it melts into liquid\nwater."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49400,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Keep heating and it boils into gas - water\nvapor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52280,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve been over this stuff before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54960,
    "dDurationMs": 4619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you watched our episode on states of matter\nyou’ll know it’s a bit more complicated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59579,
    "dDurationMs": 1041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60620,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, it’s not just temperature that determines the state of matter, it's also pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66130,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reason solids melt and liquids boil is\nthat rising heat energy allows the bonds between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71540,
    "dDurationMs": 2579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atoms and molecules to break."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74119,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But high pressure helps to keep particles\ntogether so that it takes more heat energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78369,
    "dDurationMs": 1201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to break bonds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79570,
    "dDurationMs": 4799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s why water remains a liquid at 400\ndegrees celsius near hydrothermal vents on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84369,
    "dDurationMs": 5241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ocean floor, while it boils at only 68\ncelsius on top of Mount Everest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89610,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The relationship between temperature, pressure,\nand phase is mapped on a phase diagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95710,
    "dDurationMs": 6949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, maps tend to be covered by nation states\nor states of the union, but this map has states"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102659,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103659,
    "dDurationMs": 6021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And these also have boundaries, the crossing\nof which means changing state - say, by increasing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109680,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "temperature through solid to liquid to gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112780,
    "dDurationMs": 7100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At these melting and boiling boundaries the\nmaterial is momentarily a mix of both states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119880,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are some surprises here too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122630,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, at low pressures there’s a\nboundary where increasing temperature takes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126590,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you directly from solid to gas - we call that\nsublimation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131110,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’s the so-called triple point,\nwhere all three states can exist simultaneously,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137040,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the triple point, if we increase both\ntemperature and pressure in the right proportions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141690,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we follow the phase transition boundary between\nliquid and gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145770,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we’re careful, our fluid remains in an\nequilibrium state, simultaneously boiling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150680,
    "dDurationMs": 1449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and condensing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152129,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But eventually we reach this spot, where the\nboundary appears to end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157769,
    "dDurationMs": 6471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's almost like the map gives out - as though\nour explorer experimentalists haven’t traveled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164240,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that far across the realms of pressure and\ntemperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166580,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s not the case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168920,
    "dDurationMs": 5399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This end point of the liquid-gas boundary\nis called the critical point, and it’s very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174319,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175319,
    "dDurationMs": 5241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And beyond it is an entirely new state of matter - the here-be-dragons of the phase diagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180560,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a land with no boundaries - a sort of no-man's land where liquids can skirt around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186440,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the phase boundary and become gasses without\never boiling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191250,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that region, matter becomes a sort of hybrid\nbetween liquid and gas called a supercritical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197210,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluid, sharing properties of both."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199460,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this is a genuine, unique state of matter\nthat can do things possible for neither liquid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206200,
    "dDurationMs": 1110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nor gases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207310,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What does it even look like to transition\nbetween states without ever crossing a phase"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211530,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transition boundary?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212670,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or to be a combination of liquid and gas?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215060,
    "dDurationMs": 1950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, I’m going to show you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217010,
    "dDurationMs": 5179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But first, to understand what we’re seeing,\nwe need to remind ourselves of the fundamental"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222189,
    "dDurationMs": 3671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties defining liquids and gasses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225860,
    "dDurationMs": 4129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To start with, both are fluids - they flow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229989,
    "dDurationMs": 5351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They can’t hold a rigid structure like solids\ncan, rather they take on the shape of thxeir"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235340,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "container."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236340,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A key property differentiating liquids and\ngases is their inclination to change their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241230,
    "dDurationMs": 1810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own volume or density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243040,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Liquids, for example, are remarkably difficult\nto compress in volume by the application of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247310,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "external pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248310,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That enables their use in things like hydraulic\npresses, where they can be used to exert enormous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254000,
    "dDurationMs": 1610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outward pressures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255610,
    "dDurationMs": 6761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say that liquids are incompressible. In some cases they're less compressible than their own solids. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262371,
    "dDurationMs": 5849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the volume of liquid doesn’t change even in the absence of external pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268220,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The particles of a liquid are loosely bound\nto each other, which manifests as a surface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273180,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tension and results in liquids having a distinct\nsurface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277330,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That pulls liquids into spherical blobs in\nfreefall or in the absence of gravity, while"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282190,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under gravity they take the shape of the bottom\nof their container."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286310,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While pressure doesn’t influence liquid\nvolume, temperature does."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290470,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Liquids expand with heat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293400,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gases on the other hand will always expand\nto occupy the entirety of their container,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297780,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and are also relatively easy to compress by\nexerting pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302000,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gas particles zip around without significantly\ninteracting with each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306530,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They keep moving until they hit a container\nwall."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309500,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those impacts exert a pressure on the container."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313440,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Expand the container and the gas will expand,\nbut it’ll take longer for each particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317530,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to travel between walls, so pressure drops;\nvice versa if you shrink the container."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323650,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Increase temperature and the gas particles\nmove faster, hit harder, again increasing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328340,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329340,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a so-called ideal gas, which has no inter-particle\nforces, its behavior can be described by a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335990,
    "dDurationMs": 7290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simple relationship between pressure, temperature\nand volume given by the Ideal Gas Law: PV=NkT."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343280,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, that’s about enough blah pressure temperature\nblah blah."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348780,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know what we need to know, to take a journey\nto that strange new land of the phase diagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354960,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see what it looks like for a liquid\nto pass into the supercritical phase."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360300,
    "dDurationMs": 6940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re going to do that with the help of\nNigel Braun of the NileRed and NileBlue YouTube"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367240,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "channels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368240,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I suggest you watch the entire video yourself,\nbut with Nigel’s kind permission we’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372930,
    "dDurationMs": 2530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "show you the highlights."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375460,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a pressure chamber containing dry\nice - carbon dioxide in solid form."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381550,
    "dDurationMs": 8610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That ice was presumably created below the\n194.7 Kelvin freezing point of CO2 at atmospheric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390160,
    "dDurationMs": 1100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391260,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now at room temperature it begins to sublimate\ndirectly into a gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396400,
    "dDurationMs": 6430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At atmospheric pressure, liquid CO2 can’t\nexist, but in the small volume of the chamber,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402830,
    "dDurationMs": 4869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sublimation causes pressure to rise, taking\nus into the realm of the phase diagram where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407699,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liquid CO2 is possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410199,
    "dDurationMs": 4601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That liquid fills the bottom of the chamber,\nwhile the top of the chamber is mostly filled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414800,
    "dDurationMs": 2799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with CO2 gas at a much lower density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417599,
    "dDurationMs": 5771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To reach supercriticality, we need to get\nto this part of the diagram, so both pressure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423370,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and temperature need to increase."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425740,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Conveniently enough it’s possible to get\nthere just by adding heat, and Nigel does"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430830,
    "dDurationMs": 1649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that with a hairdryer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432479,
    "dDurationMs": 5391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the temperature rises the pressure of the\ngas increases a proportional amount, but this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437870,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "temperature is also causing thermal expansion\nin the liquid, which reduces the volume of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443400,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gas, which further increases the pressure\nall around."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447830,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At first we might think this pressure has\nno effect because liquids are incompressible,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452110,
    "dDurationMs": 8010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but look at the phase diagram: At higher pressures\nthe boiling point of CO2 increases, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460120,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means all this pressure makes it harder and\nharder for the liquid to evaporate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466860,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we have a sort of feedback loop where\nthe liquid wants to transition to a gas, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471300,
    "dDurationMs": 3139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the more it tries the less it is allowed to\ndo so."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474439,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We remain stuck following the transition boundary,\nupwards in temperature and pressure, trapped"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479849,
    "dDurationMs": 2091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our state by thermodynamics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481940,
    "dDurationMs": 6699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we’re approaching our strange new region\nof the phase diagram, where the rules are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488639,
    "dDurationMs": 2731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to change"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491370,
    "dDurationMs": 2010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's talk about density for a moment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493380,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Since the gas is being compressed but its\nmass is not changing by much, its density"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498750,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must increase."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500310,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meanwhile the liquid is expanding in volume,\nso its density drops."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504460,
    "dDurationMs": 2790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eventually the density of the gas and the\nliquid become the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507250,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At that point, microscopic droplets of remaining\nliquid are free to flow and swirl through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513760,
    "dDurationMs": 2459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gas phase to diffuse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516219,
    "dDurationMs": 3021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re now at the critical point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519240,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then all the CO2 goes supercritical, and\nappears to vanish - it becomes perfectly transparent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526920,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From its visual appearance the supercritical\nCO2 could easily be a gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532070,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And really, it is a lot like a gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534050,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It fills its container and is compressible,\nand doesn’t have the surface tension of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538230,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a liquid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539510,
    "dDurationMs": 6610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The viscosity of supercritical fluids is extremely low, so they flows and diffuse more like a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546120,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas than a liquid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547990,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it has the density of a liquid, and that\nleads to behaviors not seen in gases, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553390,
    "dDurationMs": 1629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll come back to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555019,
    "dDurationMs": 4481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The high density of a supercritical fluid\nmeans that its particles do interact with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559500,
    "dDurationMs": 2670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other, unlike an ideal gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562170,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the supercritical equation of state\nis much more complex than the ideal gas law."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568110,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To demonstrate the hybrid nature of this stuff,\nNigel put some silica beads into the chamber."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573460,
    "dDurationMs": 6450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On shaking the chamber the beads move only\nslightly, like they’re under a liquid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579910,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But now with only a gas in the chamber they rattle around as expected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583320,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, let’s reverse this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586000,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When ice is applied to the chamber, temperature\ndrops and CO2 liquid rapidly condenses from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591670,
    "dDurationMs": 1630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the supercritical phase."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593300,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, if the chamber is opened\nthen pressure drops, and the supercritical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598470,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluid transforms straight into a gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601830,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the most useful property of the supercritical\nfluid is its ability to dissolve stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608510,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Due to its high density compared to a gas,\nit has more atoms or molecules to bond to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613450,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the dissolved substance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615390,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it still moves like a gas, so it can flow\nand diffuse into places that liquids can’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621430,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "access."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622430,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One very familiar application of this super-solvent\nis decaffeinated coffee."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627860,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you put coffee beans into a bath of supercritical\nCO2, the fluid will diffuse into the beans"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633750,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a gas, and bonding with the naturally\nsoluble caffeine and then diffusing out again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639520,
    "dDurationMs": 7350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then the CO2 can be brought back to gas form,\nleaving a decaffeinated bean and a fine powder"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646870,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of caffeine molecules - I guess for the energy\ndrink that you need after your decaffeinated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651970,
    "dDurationMs": 1281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coffee didn’t work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653251,
    "dDurationMs": 5329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here's another one.  The lowest density solids in the universe, Aerogels are made the same way. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658580,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A gel is a molecular scaffold full of water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662430,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you try to dry the gel it shrinks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665310,
    "dDurationMs": 4529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s because when water evaporates from\nthe surface, capillary action draws water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669839,
    "dDurationMs": 1190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the interior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671029,
    "dDurationMs": 4331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That causes an inward tension on the scaffold,\nand so it collapses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675360,
    "dDurationMs": 4969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you put the gel in a supercritical\nCO2 bath, that fluid will replace the water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680329,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then you can turn the CO2 back into a low-density gas phase, leaving the gel lattice intact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686889,
    "dDurationMs": 1378,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and full of air."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688267,
    "dDurationMs": 5183,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These aerogels have a ton of applications, but by far\nthe most interesting is to send it out into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693450,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outerspace to catch cosmic dust, as in NASA’s Stardust mission."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697830,
    "dDurationMs": 1629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What else."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699459,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ever had something drycleaned?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701279,
    "dDurationMs": 5271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, supercritical CO2 is used to dissolve\nstuff - in this case whatever’s making your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706550,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clothes filthy - but without the risk of getting\nyour precious, I dunno, hand-dyed silk pyjamas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712550,
    "dDurationMs": 1510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wet with actual water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714060,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are countless applications of supercritical\nfluids, even beyond the many applications"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718500,
    "dDurationMs": 1110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a solvent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719610,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example they’re increasingly important\nin materials science for their ability to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723670,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deposit dissolved elements for growing nano-scale\nparticles and layers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728470,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re great at moving heat energy around\ndue to their high density and corresponding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732490,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heat capacity, so we are seeing more and more application as the working fluids in power"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738550,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plants and heat pumps, and as refrigerants."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742570,
    "dDurationMs": 5949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Industrial applications often use supercritical\ncarbon dioxide, but supercritical hydrocarbons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748519,
    "dDurationMs": 4791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also see a lot of use, and supercritical water is important also."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753310,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's good for all those things you want water for - electrolysis, hydrolysis, oxidation, but when you want to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759100,
    "dDurationMs": 5179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "avoid actual wetness, or you need it to flow\nand diffuse like a gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764279,
    "dDurationMs": 5631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, good for us - we discovered supercritical\nfluids and you can now sip your decaf latte"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769910,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in your nicely drycleaned silk pyjamas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772790,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Nature knew about this state of matter\nlong before we did."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777460,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unlike other exotic states, supercritical\nfluids can be found on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781620,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or, rather, in the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783810,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Water circulating deep below ground in geothermally\nactive areas can reach the high temperatures"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789380,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and pressures needed for a supercritical state,\nand even, albeit rarely, emerge in that state"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795360,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in hydrothermal vents on the ocean floor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797940,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s rare on Earth, but if you lived on\nVenus you wouldn’t think of the supercritical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802610,
    "dDurationMs": 2550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluid as a fringe state of matter at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805160,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Venusian atmosphere is almost entirely\ncarbon dioxide, creating a greenhouse effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810500,
    "dDurationMs": 6029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that heats the surface to more than 700 K.\nCombined with the extreme atmospheric pressure,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816529,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this puts CO2 within a few kilometers of the surface well beyond the supercritical point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822709,
    "dDurationMs": 5101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We often think of Venus as a rocky planet, but it’s also fair to think of it as an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827810,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ocean world - an ocean of supercritical fluid\nthat I don’t recommend you try swimming"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833850,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834850,
    "dDurationMs": 3549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jupiter, Saturn, and probably the other gas\ngiants are also supercritical ocean worlds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838399,
    "dDurationMs": 1161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839560,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They have solid cores of rock and ice, in\nthe case of Jupter and Saturn surrounded by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844410,
    "dDurationMs": 6970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solid metallic hydrogen, and then a thick\nlayer of supercritical fluid - mostly hydrogen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851380,
    "dDurationMs": 6009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of that is buried beneath extremely thick\natmospheres of gas-phase mostly-hydrogen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857389,
    "dDurationMs": 4231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the supercritical layer is an integral\npart of the structure of the biggest planets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861620,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the solar system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862640,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, let’s review all of the states of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866060,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Solids liquids gases plasmas, bose-einstein\ncondensates and the resulting superconductors"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871610,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and superfluids; nuclear matter of various\ntypes; photonic matter; various spin-based"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876750,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states from ferromagnets to quantum spin liquids\nto time crystals; come to think of it, let’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881660,
    "dDurationMs": 3549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not review all the states of matter, because\nI’m starting to think there may be no end"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885209,
    "dDurationMs": 3361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the weird ways that matter can be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888570,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of these are total edge cases, but some,\nlike supercritical fluids, are incredibly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893870,
    "dDurationMs": 2030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and increasingly useful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895900,
    "dDurationMs": 7510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And while this stuff is rare on this mostly wet and gassy rock, supercritical fluids are surprisingly abundant in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903410,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other parts of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905780,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d like to thank Speakly for supporting\nPBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909110,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If time travel were invented and you thought\n“Great!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911910,
    "dDurationMs": 3510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now I can go back to the 1920’s and debate\ninterpretations of quantum mechanics with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915420,
    "dDurationMs": 1349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein and Schrödinger!”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916769,
    "dDurationMs": 2401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– well, then you’d need to learn German."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919170,
    "dDurationMs": 4409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unless you already know it… but even in\nthis current time period, if you’ve wanted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923579,
    "dDurationMs": 4471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to learn another language, then I’d like\nto introduce you to Speakly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928050,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Offering 8 different languages to choose from,\nSpeakly was created by 2 polyglots who both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932529,
    "dDurationMs": 1631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speak 7 languages."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934160,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They researched thousands of language learners\nand created a unique method that teaches words"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939610,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sentences based on their relevance in\nreal-life situations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943160,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This means that you don’t learn anything\nthat you actually can’t use to speak the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947970,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "language."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948970,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Available on both web and mobile platforms,\nyou’ll learn new vocabulary, with speaking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953500,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exercises, writing exercises, listening comprehension\nexercises, and even music recommendations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958600,
    "dDurationMs": 3099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the language that you’re learning so\nthat you don’t get bored."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961699,
    "dDurationMs": 2551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a link in the description to learn\nmore."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964250,
    "dDurationMs": 3779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get to comments, I wanted to let\nyou know that there will be an end of year"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968029,
    "dDurationMs": 4511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AMA next Monday at 4pm EST on the channel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972540,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll post more information on the community\ntab and we look forward to hearing your questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978460,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This will be the last content before we take a holiday break."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981910,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re doing comment responses for\nthe last two episodes: the one on quasiparticles,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986380,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the one where we talked about the recent\ndetection of neutrinos coming from a supermassive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990220,
    "dDurationMs": 1059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991279,
    "dDurationMs": 3441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with quasiparticles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994720,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some Kid and Cybernatural ask whether quasiparticles\ncould be used to explain dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000270,
    "dDurationMs": 6450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is almost certainly unfortunately no. Quasiparticles require some sort of non-elementary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006720,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field to exist in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008000,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, they exist in fields that\narise from a volume being filled with matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013940,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter suffuses the near-vacuum of space,\nwhere we mostly just have the elementary quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019480,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020480,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Excitations in those fields are proper elementary\nparticles, not quasiparticles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025220,
    "dDurationMs": 5829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Amorphant points out that we didn’t mention\nmagnetic monopoles as an example of quasiparticles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031049,
    "dDurationMs": 3731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reason we didn’t is that I didn’t\nknow that these things had been created as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1034780,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quasiparticles - so thanks for the heads up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038140,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actual magnetic monopoles, if they really\nexist, would be elementary particles, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042630,
    "dDurationMs": 2309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ve done an episode on these before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1044939,
    "dDurationMs": 4121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it turns out they can also be created\nas quasiparticles by separating the poles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049060,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a regular dipole magnetic field, for example\nby careful manipulation of spins in a crystal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055140,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lattice."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056140,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Check out Felix Flicker’s Royal Institute\ntalk for an example."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1059950,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Link below."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060950,
    "dDurationMs": 5229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dr. Lisa Schumacher of the ice cube collaboration\ndropped into the comments to say hi, and to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066179,
    "dDurationMs": 4631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let us know that the update northern-sky neutrino\nmap is available."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070810,
    "dDurationMs": 2369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ve put the link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073179,
    "dDurationMs": 5811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So crazy to me that an observatory at the\nsouth pole observes the northern hemisphere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078990,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sky because it needs the Earth to be in the\nway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082630,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, thanks for stopping by, Dr. Schumacher,\nand for all the hard work of you and your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088210,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "team."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089210,
    "dDurationMs": 6079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GNGR11 asks whether it’s possible to detect\nand map the cosmic neutrino background like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095289,
    "dDurationMs": 2601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what was done with the cosmic microwave background."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097890,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So first, the cosmic neutrino background refers\nto neutrinos that were created at around one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103400,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "second after the big bang, when the density\nbecame low enough for neutrinos to travel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108470,
    "dDurationMs": 1089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "freely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109559,
    "dDurationMs": 5951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These neutrinos would now have energies 10\nbillion times smaller than regular neutrinos,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1115510,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say from the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1117090,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That makes them way harder to detect, and\nwe’ve never positively identified one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1122490,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are experiments currently being built\nto try to detect these, but they won’t be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1127360,
    "dDurationMs": 3679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to measure the direction the neutrinos\ncome from."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1131039,
    "dDurationMs": 6281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Making making an actual map sounds extraordinarily\ndifficult, and may not be possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137320,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "SomeRandomGuy asks What would our night sky\nlook like if our galactic centre had a Quasar?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1144440,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Great question!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1145440,
    "dDurationMs": 4219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I don't know. Well I do know now because I figured that out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1149659,
    "dDurationMs": 4801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quasars or “active galactic nuclei” glow\nfrom the heat energy of matter being ripped"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154460,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to shreds as it spirals towards a supermassive\nblack hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158950,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to Arthur Eddington, there’s a\nmaximum brightness for an object like this,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1163820,
    "dDurationMs": 4469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "determined by the maximum amount of fuel you\ncan pour in before the outgoing radiation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168289,
    "dDurationMs": 2451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stalls the fuel supply."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1170740,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The limit for the Milky Way’s black hole\nis around 100-billion times brighter than"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1175760,
    "dDurationMs": 1540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1177300,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Factor in the relative distance of the center\nof the Milky Way and you get that a Milky"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1182200,
    "dDurationMs": 7690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Way quasar shining at the Eddington limit would be about 3 times brighter on our sky"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1189890,
    "dDurationMs": 1690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compared to the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1191580,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would also have a much harder spectrum\n- so a lot more nasty UV light, not to mention"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1197070,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "x-rays and gamma rays, which may wreak havoc\non the ozone layer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1201520,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not all active galaxies glow at the maximum,\nand there are regions of such galaxies that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1208000,
    "dDurationMs": 7390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are shielded by the thick wall of dust surrounding\nthe whirlpool, so the number I gave is worse-case"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1215390,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scenario."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1216390,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Still, probably not something we should be\nhoping for."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1219890,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And happily it’s also not something that can happen\nin the next few billion years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1224830,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Harmony Smurf and weksauce echo my warning\nabout seeing flashes in your eye: It’s almost"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1230870,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "certainly not a neutrino!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1232470,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Go see a doctor!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1234350,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Apparently migraine auras and virtreous detachment\nare a common cause."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1240590,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A retina detachment is a much rarer possibility,\nbut not as rare as seeing Cherenkov radiation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1246600,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from neutrinos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1248200,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Still, this would make an awesome House episode,\nright?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1251960,
    "dDurationMs": 2050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And I would be willing to do a cameo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1254010,
    "dDurationMs": 4331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ve always wanted to be a consulting astrophysicist\nat a hospital."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1258341,
    "dDurationMs": 2338,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’d finally feel like a real doctor."
    } ]
  } ]
}
